  ## 3. Subsystem Breakdown and Functionality
  ### 3.1 Networking & Wire Protocol (core/networking/)
  • MessageFrame: Network packets sent over UDP cannot be arbitrary blobs. Every packet is wrapped in a strict binary frame:
      • Magic Bytes: 0x44494350 ("DICP" in ASCII) to discard noise/foreign packets.
      • Protocol Version (1 byte), Message Type (2 bytes), Payload Length (4 bytes), and CRC32 Checksum (4 bytes).
      • Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) enforcement: Any UDP datagram larger than 1200 bytes is rejected to prevent IP fragmentation over WAN.
  • UdpTransport: Implements non-blocking, asynchronous UDP sockets using Boost.Asio. Handles continuous reading and queued asynchronous sending without blocking the thread.
  • TcpTransport: Length-prefixed framed TCP transport used when two nodes perform bulk Anti-Entropy Synchronization (transferring large membership lists and service tables that exceed UDP MTU).
  ### 3.2 Cluster Membership & SWIM Failure Detection (core/membership/, core/gossip/)
  • NodeIdentity: Represents a node's identity in the cluster. It holds a unique Node ID, an ephemeral Boot UUID (generated afresh on every startup), network ports, and an atomic Incarnation Number (used for refuting false rumors).
  • MembershipManager: Maintains the local view of all nodes in the cluster and executes deterministic conflict resolution:
      • Rule 1 (Higher Incarnation Wins): If an update arrives with an incarnation number higher than our local record, the higher incarnation always wins.
      • Rule 2 (Equal Incarnation Precedence): When incarnation numbers are equal, the state machine resolves strictly as:
    DEAD > SUSPECT > ALIVE
    Graceful departure (LEFT) is an absorbing final state.

  • FailureDetector (SWIM Protocol):
      1. Direct Ping: Node A randomly picks Node B and sends a MSG_PING.
      2. Indirect Ping-Req: If Node B does not answer within probe_timeout_ms (e.g. 80ms), Node A selects k other random peers (C, D) and sends MSG_PING_REQ. Nodes C and D attempt to ping B on A's behalf.
      3. Suspicion State: If nobody can reach B, Node B is not immediately killed. Instead, it is marked SUSPECT and a suspicion timer starts.
      4. Refutation: If Node B is actually alive (e.g. its link to A was temporarily slow), B hears the rumor that it is SUSPECT. B immediately refutes this by incrementing its incarnation number (I_{new} = I_{old} + 1) and
      broadcasting ALIVE with the new incarnation.
      5. Dead Declaration: If Node B never refutes within suspicion_timeout_ms, it transitions to DEAD.
  • GossipEngine & DisseminationQueue:
      • Implements randomized peer fanout (f = 3).
      • Instead of broadcasting state changes to every server (which creates O(N²) network flood), updates are pushed to a priority queue and piggybacked on regular gossip pings to random peers.
      • Deduplication Cache: Remembers recently seen message UUIDs so identical messages are never processed or forwarded twice.
      • Fast Bootstrap: When a new node joins and contacts a seed, the seed replies immediately with its full membership view, allowing convergence in 1 RTT.


  ### 3.3 Replicated Service Registry (core/registry/)
  • ServiceRegistry: Manages service instances (e.g., payment-service at 10.0.0.5:8080).
  • Heartbeat Leases: Every service registration has a Time-To-Live (TTL, default 10 seconds). Services must periodically renew their lease.
  • Lease Expiration: If a service crashes and stops renewing, CheckLeaseExpirations() flags it as expired.
  • Tombstones: In an eventually consistent gossip network, simply deleting a key causes bugs (an older gossip packet from a lagging peer could resurrect the deleted service). DICP replaces expired services with a Tombstone for 60
  seconds. Peers learn that the service was explicitly deleted. After 60 seconds, the tombstone is safely purged.

  ### 3.4 Active Health Monitoring (core/health/)

  • HealthMonitor: Decouples the physical machine's health from the service's health.
  • Runs an asynchronous Boost.Beast HTTP client that polls the /health endpoint of registered services.
  • Hysteresis Filtering:
      • Requires N consecutive failures (e.g., 3) before marking a service UNHEALTHY.
      • Requires M consecutive successes (e.g., 2) before restoring it to HEALTHY.
      • Prevents "flapping" (services flickering between up and down due to transient hiccups).

  ### 3.5 Asynchronous Data Plane & HTTP Load Balancer (core/load_balancer/)

  • BackendSnapshot: An immutable snapshot containing only healthy, eligible backend endpoints. When services change in the control plane, a new snapshot is constructed and swapped into the proxy.
  • HttpProxy: An asynchronous HTTP reverse proxy built on Boost.Beast.
      • Request path is completely lock-free:
        auto snap = GetCurrentSnapshot(); // std::atomic_load of std::shared_ptr
	auto endpoints = snap->GetEndpoints(service_name);

      • Incoming requests are proxied directly to selected backends with zero registry lock contention.
      • Includes administrative endpoints:
	  • POST /admin/snapshot: Ingests an updated routing snapshot dynamically.
	  • POST /admin/strategy: Dynamically switches load balancing algorithms without restarting the process.
	  • /metrics: Native Prometheus exporter for proxy throughput and error counts.

  • 4 Load Balancing Strategies:
      1. Round Robin: Cycles through backends uniformly using atomic integer addition.
      2. Weighted Round Robin: Respects backend capacity weights (e.g., 5:3:2) using smooth deficit round-robin.
      3. Least Connections: Inspects active_connections.load() across backends in real time and routes to the backend currently processing the fewest active requests.
      4. Consistent Hashing: Hashes request keys (e.g. X-Routing-Key header or user ID) across a 100-virtual-node ring. Guarantees that the same user/session always routes to the exact same backend server, with minimal key disruption
      if a backend joins or leaves.


  ### 3.6 Distributed Configuration Management (core/config_manager/)

  • ConfigurationManager: Distributes configuration key-values across the cluster.
  • Hierarchical Scopes: Supports GLOBAL, DATACENTER, NODE, and SERVICE scopes.
  • Monotonic Versioning: Every update increments a 64-bit version number. Stale updates with older or equal versions are deterministically rejected.
  • Auditable Rollback: Reverting a configuration does not delete history. It writes a new higher version number containing the historical payload, ensuring clean forward replication.
  ### 3.7 Durability & Persistence (core/storage/)

  • SqliteStorage: Manages an embedded SQLite database on each node.
  • Configured with PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) and PRAGMA synchronous = NORMAL, allowing concurrent reads while writes take place.
  • All SQL operations use compiled prepared statements with typed bindings (sqlite3_bind_*), preventing SQL injection.
  • Crash Recovery: When a node crashes (e.g., power loss or SIGKILL) and restarts, it reads its saved records from SQLite, increments its persisted incarnation number (I ← I + 1), and restarts cleanly without state loss.
  ### 3.8 Management API (management/)
  • Written in Python 3.12 using FastAPI and Pydantic v2.
  • Exposes authenticated REST endpoints (/api/v1/cluster/members, /api/v1/cluster/services/register, /api/v1/cluster/config).
  • Secured via HTTP Bearer token authentication verified with constant-time comparison (secrets.compare_digest) to prevent timing attacks.
  ### 3.9 Observability & Monitoring (monitoring/)

  • Prometheus: Native scraping of Node Agent management ports (:9002) and Load Balancer metrics (:80).
  • Alert Rules (cluster_alerts.yml): Pre-configured alert rules for NodeMarkedDead, NodeUnderSuspicion, and NoHealthyBackends.
  • Grafana: Pre-provisioned dashboards visualizing active node count, healthy service instances, gossip transmission rates, and proxy latency percentiles.
